        <div><a name="sec-StripsAndPatches" id="sec-StripsAndPatches"></a><h2>4.27<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Strips and Patches</h2>

<div align="center" class="table_container"><table class="MsoISOTable" style="mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;border-spacing:0;border-width:1px;"><a name="_e2caf292-1a38-892d-1666-46604c77655b" id="_e2caf292-1a38-892d-1666-46604c77655b"></a><tbody><tr><td valign="top" align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;"><p style="text-align: left;page-break-after:avoid" class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5ba578b0-2f3a-4a3c-2106-e8e16682a210" id="_5ba578b0-2f3a-4a3c-2106-e8e16682a210"></a><u><b>IHO Definition</b> :</u> A strip is a long narrow area of floating ice, about 1 kilometre or less in width, usually composed of small fragments detached from the main mass of ice, and run together under the influence of wind, swell or current. If the area of ice becomes more rounded in shape, it is referred to as a patch.</p>
</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;page-break-after:avoid;"><p style="text-align: left;page-break-after:avoid" class="MsoNormal"><a name="_716ea59f-8ee2-6e1e-efa7-6db62ed7d809" id="_716ea59f-8ee2-6e1e-efa7-6db62ed7d809"></a><u><b>S-411 Geo Feature:</b></u> Strips and Patches</p>
</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;page-break-after:auto;"><p style="text-align: left;page-break-after:auto" class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9e34cdb5-b774-b879-46ec-11db8387fab3" id="_9e34cdb5-b774-b879-46ec-11db8387fab3"></a><u><b>Primitives</b>: point</u></p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

