        <div><a name="sec-directionofSastrugi" id="sec-directionofSastrugi"></a><h2>5.4<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Direction of Sastrugi</h2>

<div align="center" class="table_container"><table class="MsoISOTable" style="mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;border-spacing:0;border-width:1px;"><a name="_056034d6-4212-234b-781c-0344ae218888" id="_056034d6-4212-234b-781c-0344ae218888"></a><tbody><tr><td valign="top" align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;page-break-after:auto;"><p style="text-align: left;page-break-after:auto" class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c36cbe7f-53c6-9bda-cc8a-ce5759791150" id="_c36cbe7f-53c6-9bda-cc8a-ce5759791150"></a><u><b>IHO Definition</b> :</u> ICEDOS indicates the bearing of a sastrugi.  Sastrugi are sharp, irregular ridges formed on a snow surface by wind erosion and deposition. On mobile floating ice the ridges are parallel to the direction of the wind at the time they were formed.</p>

<p style="text-align: left;page-break-after:auto" class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e36f4478-a405-dcb6-20fe-644b6fe66bb2" id="_e36f4478-a405-dcb6-20fe-644b6fe66bb2"></a><u><b> Attribute Type :</b></u> enumeration</p>

